+#if defined(TARGET_SWITCH) || defined(TARGET_MORTAR)
+/* Vertex ring (Switch; Mortar too: on the PowerVR stock driver every glBufferData allocates and
+ * maps new device memory, 35% of the frame at 4 fps in the title demo). glBufferData per draw makes Mesa replace the buffer storage, and freeing a
  * nouveau bo whose fence was already flushed (any buffer the previous frame drew from) blocks the
  * CPU until the GPU has finished that frame (libdrm_nouveau nouveau_bo_del -> nvFenceWait), so the
  * CPU and the GPU never work at the same time (diagnosed in SwitchWaker's SWITCH_PERF_STUDY for the
  * same switch-mesa 20.1). Instead: one 8 MiB buffer written front to back through unsynchronised
  * maps; draws address their vertices with a base vertex. A fence per quarter guards reuse after a
  * wrap (a frame streams well under 1 MiB, so the waits only matter if the GPU is frames behind). */
+#ifndef TARGET_MORTAR /* Mortar: GLES 3.2 core, loaded with the rest */
